# GitHub Issues & Projects Synchronization Rule

Follow this workflow whenever planning tasks, features, refactors, or bugs that involve creating or managing GitHub issues and project boards.

## 1. Plan & Approval Gate (Mandatory)
- **Never create or modify GitHub issues/projects autonomously.**
- Present a clear, structured plan to the user first, including:
  - Proposed issue title(s)
  - Detailed description with background/context
  - Actionable markdown checklist of acceptance criteria (`- [ ]`)
  - Relevant labels and milestone (if applicable)
  - Target project board
- Wait for explicit user confirmation or edits before running any `gh` commands.

## 2. Default Assignee Requirement
- **Always assign created issues to the user:**
  - Pass `--assignee "@me"` on all `gh issue create` commands.
  - If creating/editing issues via script or API, ensure the authenticated user (`@me`) is included in `assignees`.

## 3. Repository & Project Auto-Discovery
- Detect the repository context using the GitHub CLI:
  ```bash
  gh repo view --json nameWithOwner,owner,name
  ```
- Identify relevant projects:
  ```bash
  # List user or repo/org projects
  gh project list
  ```

## 4. Issue Creation Standard
Create issues using `gh issue create`:
```bash
gh issue create \
  --title "<Clear, concise title>" \
  --body "<Context + Acceptance Criteria checklist>" \
  --assignee "@me" \
  --label "<label1>,<label2>"
```

### Issue Body Template:
```markdown
## Summary
<Brief description of the work and rationale>

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] <Specific testable requirement 1>
- [ ] <Specific testable requirement 2>

## References / Context
- Relates to #<issue_number>
```

## 5. GitHub Project Syncing
- Once an issue is created, immediately link it to the target project board:
  ```bash
  gh project item-create <project-number> --owner <owner> --url <issue-url>
  ```
- Keep the project board synchronized as work progresses:
  - Move items to `In Progress` when starting work.
  - Update to `Done` upon closing issues or merging associated PRs.
